ng the Halston story if you're just  going to tell the same thing over and  over again and then I saw Dior and I was  really taken by what Fred did which was  to tell the not the obvious story he  told the story about the back room and I  said I want that approach I want  somebody who take to do what to do the  Halston story in a way that no one else  has  Frederick you had done a bunch of  fashion films before as director editor  so was this something you were  interested in or did you kind of have to  get talked into it  I gave Roland a little bit of a hard  time in the beginning because I was  eager to sort of do something else in  fashion because you know I love I love  fashion I've I've I've loved all the  stories I've told in fashion but I was  you know I was telling Rowan what's the  biggest story beyond fashion and I think  deeper on a deeper level it after making  Joe and I I realized that I wanted to  talk about the place of creative people  within an industry I think I had been at  a board room table with corporate people  and realized that was very alone that I  was speaking a totally different  language and that people I was not  getting through to people were not  interested in making you know creative  decisions or you know a creative product  they were just interested in the bottom  line and so Halston story completely  resonated with me and devastated me I  was like wow this is the little bit that  I've lived through you know times a  thousand and and I wanted to understand  AHA that happened you know the more I  read the more complex the story got  yeah well it's such a complicated story  but I think he came up with a great sort  of structural device with the narrator  who's digging through the tapes the you  know fictional character I'm curious  where you came up with that idea and how  early in the process and what your  thinking was there I think it was pretty  early I mean I think it was really going  with my instinct that this was a very  big 