y just for  me I just now we shouldn't put score  there it should be a song or nothing you  know so and the source pieces in this  piece of right on spot on so and we were  able to use source music from you to  fake a fifty four five studio 54 scene  is basically a track 477 yes I mean no  it's 79 maybe it's a while ago well the  music really just unifies the whole  thing and it's it's a very seamless  smooth ride this movie but again so  complicated I'm curious what the editing  process was like how much trial and  error was there getting to this version  how many different versions were there  there are there are many versions I mean  mostly because this is a CNN film so we  have to do TV versions so that opened up  that that teaches you how to compress  your film and then you learn things and  you're like oh maybe we should use that  but also we did so with the the film we  show that Sundance is slightly longer  and we're going to release that on  Amazon I think later this year it's  gonna be the director's cut  quote-unquote and I mean I'm probably  the only one who keeps track of all the  different versions in terms of like  what's in it what's not but it was a  very very long editing process because I  really wanted it to play as you say like  really sort of seamlessly and because  we're working with so many different  parts so many interviews and then a few  scripted scenes and archival it was very  important for me that it became just one  thing and that took time to actually to  work out I was really impressed on the  section that Fred did for that  introduces Norton Simon the Liza  Minnelli song yes because that was a  case where he took a song which totally  embodied what he was trying to say and  made it the centerpiece and yet we've  done all this other historical archive  all the interviews and that was the that  was the one I mean it's all beautifully  edited but that was one scene that  really struck me and then I was so happy  were able to clear tha